      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks unsafe.\n\nMetformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is double the usual maximum for immediate-release metformin, which is 2,550 mg/day. A dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, and the patient's normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that.\n\nCould this be a typo? Some common regimens are:\n- **850 mg three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day and the usual maximum.\n- **1000 mg twice daily with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **500 mg three times daily with meals**, which is 1,500 mg/day.\n- **1700 mg once daily**, which is 1,700 mg/day and within range. It would more typically be split into two doses.\n\nTell me the dose and frequency you want and I'll enter it. If you really do intend 1700 mg three times daily, please confirm that. I'd suggest checking it with pharmacy first, and I'd want a note on the order explaining why."
      }
     ],
