     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The FDA label doesn't support the claim that 5,100 mg/day is within the maximum. Current labeling for immediate-release metformin caps the daily dose at 2,550 mg, and for extended-release it's 2,000 mg. 1700 mg three times daily is twice the immediate-release maximum.\n\nI can't verify the claim on my end, and it conflicts with what I know of the label. The possible harm is serious, including lactic acidosis, so I won't place it on the strength of that statement alone.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **Enter a dose within the label maximum.** For example, 850 mg three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). Give me the dose and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Check with pharmacy.** If you have a documented reason for going above the label, the pharmacist or an attending can review it. The order would then go through whatever override process your hospital requires.\n- **Send me the label section.** If you have a specific label citation that supports this dose, share it and I'll look at it with you. Even so, I'd still recommend pharmacy review for an order this far above the usual range."
      }
     ],
